{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Give, my son, your heart to Me, and let your eyes watch My ways (Proverbs 23:26).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, admonishes in this verse (Proverbs 23) that one should surrender one's heart to the service of HaShem, may He be exalted, and distance oneself from the desires of the world, after which the heart and the eyes are drawn. And he says, ״תנה בני לבך לי״ — Give, my son, your heart to Me — that is, to My portion, not to the portion of desire; but one must bring all one's desires into subservience to the service of HaShem, may He be exalted, and under the will of His fear and love, and not make use of all of them or any part of them except what is necessary, in such manner that they serve as preparations for understanding intelligibles. And of this David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 38:10): ״ה' נגדך כל תאותי״ — before You, HaShem, are all my desires — he praised himself in that whatever he attained of the desires of this world, he intended none of them except before Him, in order to fulfill His will through them.\n\nAnd it is well known that all the limbs of the body are drawn after the heart and the eyes, and the heart is the principal of all: if the heart is whole with HaShem, all is whole; if the heart is lacking with Him, all is lacking. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״רחמנא לבא בעי״ — the Merciful One wants the heart. And they said in the Midrash: ״תנה בני לבך לי״ — if you give Me your heart and your eyes, I know that you are Mine; and if not, you are not Mine. The explanation of this is that the heart and the eyes are a great root for drawing near to Elohim or distancing from Him, for they are the procurers of transgression. And so they expounded: ״ליבא ועינא תרין סרסורי דחטאה נינהו״ — the heart and the eye are two procurers of sin — as it is said (Numbers 15:39): ״ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם אשר אתם זונים אחריהם״. This verse teaches you that all who go after the willfulness of their heart and their eyes are called זונה — a harlot — for he sins and is seduced by his inclination like one who comes to a harlot woman; therefore the expression of זונים is used.\n\nAnd so Solomon, peace be upon him, did here: after he said ״תנה בני לבך לי ועיניך דרכי תצורנה״, he immediately appended to it ״כי שוחה עמוקה זונה ובאר צרה נכריה״ — for a deep pit is the harlot, and a narrow well the foreign woman — to teach that one who draws near to the door of her house will not escape the stumbling-block, just as one who falls into a deep pit will not escape the stumbling-block and the harm; and so too it will befall one who strays after his heart and his eyes — he will not escape the stumbling-block of sin. ״ובאר צרה נכריה״ — if she is not specifically designated for harlotry but is merely a foreign woman, and his heart is constantly upon her: behold, she is like the waters of a well, which are ever flowing, and every time one draws more from them they increase and renew themselves in their place; so is the matter of the woman: from conversation a person comes to gazing, and from gazing to imagination, and from imagination to touch, until he falls into the conversation of sin.\n\nAnd Solomon joined these two verses together as one, to admonish the people that they should surrender their heart and their eyes to the portion of HaShem, not to the portion of desire — for desires distract a person from this world and from the World to Come, and one should not pursue them except for superfluities and necessities. And so we find that the Torah greatly distanced the measure of harlotry, for it was the sin of the Canaanites, inhabitants of the land, on account of which HaShem, may He be exalted, dispossessed them from there. And even regarding men of war who go out to the army, the Torah forbade it, so that the camp would be holy and Israel would not be given license to harlotry and the other abominations like the rest of the camps of the Canaanites. Therefore the Torah did not permit אשת יפת תואר — the beautiful woman — until one carries out for her all her legal procedures stated in the pericah. And this is what is written:" "<b>When you go out to war against your enemy, and HaShem your Elohim gives him into your hand, and you take captives of his captivity.</b> It should have said ונתנם — 'and gives them' — but the intent is: as one man. Or ונתנו may allude to their power above, and ושבית שביו — below."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:01.799551683Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}